# Release build matrix — the platforms the libservirtium_vcr.so/.dylib/.dll is cross-built for.
#
# All of these cross-compile from a single Linux host via `ae build --target`
# (zig cc backend) — no per-OS runner. Each maps to an output extension:
#   *-linux    -> .so      (ELF)
#   *-macos    -> .dylib   (Mach-O)
#   *-windows  -> .dll     (PE)   [slower mingw cross; opt-in]
#   *-freebsd  -> .so      [needs a per-arch base sysroot; opt-in]
#
# The core lib (libservirtium_vcr) is pure Aether + a ~12-line C string bridge, so
# building on Linux and running on the target test IDENTICAL bytes — zig
# cross-compiles the same output deterministically. What a Linux host can't do is
# RUN an arm64-macOS artifact; that's the out-of-band attestation (see README).

# Core matrix: no sysroot, fast, always built.
RELEASE_TARGETS="aarch64-linux x86_64-linux aarch64-macos x86_64-macos"

# Opt-in extras (RELEASE_EXTRA_TARGETS=1 to include). Windows cross is slow;
# FreeBSD needs a base sysroot (build.sh selects it per target arch — see below).
RELEASE_EXTRA_TARGETS_LIST="x86_64-windows aarch64-windows x86_64-freebsd"

# PARKED — aarch64-freebsd. Cross-builds against an x86_64 FreeBSD base fail with
# `unknown type name 'mcontext_t'` (sys/_ucontext.h pulls the arch-specific
# <machine/ucontext.h>), and NO aarch64 FreeBSD base is available: the aether
# release ships an x86_64-only sysroot tarball, and aether-crossbuild's deps.lock
# has no freebsd-base-aarch64-15 pin. Same wall the selenium core lib hit — tracked
# in aether asks/aether-freebsd-crossbuild-lib-and-mcontext.md item (c). build.sh
# is already per-arch-correct, so the day an aarch64 FreeBSD base exists, add the
# base and append "aarch64-freebsd" here — nothing else changes.
RELEASE_PARKED_TARGETS_LIST="aarch64-freebsd"
